me, fnerd-
> >[Protection agencies, to become states] at least have to overthrow 
> >their competitors and their clients (revenue source).

Tim Starr-
> This, of course, is the most likely set of targets for them to start
> agression against on their way to Statehood.

Right, I'm saying overthrowing all your competitors and your clients
is a big first step... Not to ignore your next point, though...

> Either all agencies agree
> to collaborate, or non-collaborators are neutralized.  Then, as the
> client population is disarmed and unschooled in providing for their
> own security, they have no alternatives to the State.

That's an interesting point.  To drift somewhat off-topic, I think that
talk about anarchy assumes the people were already somewhat schooled, 
"armed" and mobilized against the problems of centralized force.

> The question isn't whether this is a big risk or not, the question is
> whether it can be better minimized by another distribution of power.

There's also the question of what system will be supported by the market
and people's perceived costs (money but also sheer bother) and benefits 
(safety from takeover).  Or better, what kind of setup makes the true
risks, benefits and costs most visible in the market, including the true
value of that visibility (maybe it's better to be safe than sure)?

> >...Tampering with the system is coercion by (the system's) definition...
> 
> My point is that either the system has to define it as coercion, even though
> it isn't, or it has to have a natural monopoly on what's needed to tamper
> with it.  The first is evil, the second highly improbable.

The second (except for the word "natural"?) is possible in a blue goo sea, 
which is hard for *one* set of reasons.  We are assuming way-advanced tech, 
though.

The first is based on the idea that everyone agrees to this thing.  That 
wouldn't be evil if it were true.  The problem is that it's not, either for 
people alive when the decision is made, or people born after that.
I think Miller and Drexler imagine conversions from *other* force-
monopoly or -oligopoly situations, so that this is at least *less evil*
in some ways.

So, is changing the laws of nature a crime against the future?
Is it sensible to think of blue goo as the property of those it 
protects?  Property they can't get rid of?
Is it more ethical to bear a child in a world where the materials for
tools of coercion are just not available, versus a world where attempting 
to make them is stopped by force?

