From: Harvey Newstrom <hnewstrom@hnewstrom.ess.harris.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Mar 94 16:05:54 EST
Subject: [#94-3-9] BABYLON 5:  Anti-Extropian?

Has anyone else noticed an Anti-Extropian theme to the Babylon 5 stories?  

In the episode with the soul stealer the protagonist rescues souls from 
being stored in an interactive state, and releases them into the 
nothingness from which they came.  It was portrayed at the end of the 
episode as a beautiful moment as she crushed the globes one by one, killing 
each consciousness that had been stored after death.

In the episode with the biolgical technology, there were definite views 
against disturbing dead worlds (sacred burial grounds?) and trying to seek 
out "alien" technology.

In the episode with the super-psychic Chekov, they seem to state that such 
increased mental powers are somehow off-limits to humans, and we have no 
right seeking such abilities.

In the episode where each culture shows its religion to the other cultures, 
they seemed to indicate that an exchange of religion, not science, would be 
the means of achieving peace in the galaxy.  At the end, the Earthman seems 
to "best" the other cultures by having hundreds of diverse religions to 
explore.

I had hoped that Babylon 5 would be more realistic than Star Trek, as their 
portrayal of science seemed to indicate.  But now it seems that the limited 
scientific advancement is not due to scientific accuracy, but a belief that 
science is limited.  Note that the main characters seem to be psychics, 
security, and management types.  Few scientists are portrayed as positive 
models.
